{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132097",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $7\\cdot 3≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+7\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+21\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡26\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
